/**
 * @mainpage Interstellar
 *
 * @section modules_sec Modules
 *
 * @subsection graphics_sec Graphics
 * The Graphics module exposes a minimal, cross-API facade for rendering:
 * - Device setup and frame lifecycle: @ref Interstellar::Graphics::IGraphics
 * - GPU resources: @ref Interstellar::Graphics::IMesh, @ref Interstellar::Graphics::ITexture
 * - Shaders and pipelines: @ref Interstellar::Graphics::IShader, @ref Interstellar::Graphics::IRenderPipeline
 *
 * Concrete backends (OpenGL, Vulkan, DirectX12, Metal) implement these interfaces.
 *
 * #### Lifecycle
 * Typical order of operations per frame:
 * 1. Initialise() once at startup.
 * 2. BeginFrame()
 * 3. SubmitMesh(...) zero or more times
 * 4. EndFrame()
 * 5. Repeat until ShouldClose() is true, then Shutdown().
 *
 * `Resize()` can be called any time the window size changes.
 *
 * #### Threading
 * Unless stated otherwise by an implementation, all methods should be called on
 * the main/render thread. Resource creation may be expensive; prefer creating
 * and reusing resources outside of the hot path.
 *
 * #### Best practices
 * - Create meshes/textures once and reuse them across frames.
 * - Record debug names to improve GPU debugging (SetDebugName where supported).
 * - Avoid per-frame shader/pipeline creation; treat them as long-lived.
 * - Prefer indexed meshes; keep index type consistent within a backend.
 * - Gate optional features off GetAPI() when behavior must diverge per backend.
 *
 * #### API notes
 * Backends may not expose the same native handle types. Treat `GetNativeHandle()`
 * as opaque unless you are writing backend-specific code.
 *
 * See @ref Graphics "Interstellar Graphics module".
 *
 * @subsection rendererers_sec Renderers
 * 
 * The Renderers module provides an API-agnostic rendering facade and
 * concrete backends. Start with the interfaces in @ref RenderersCore,
 * then use a backend such as @ref RenderersOpenGL.
 *
 * Contents:
 * - @ref Renderers "Renderer Interfaces"
 * - @ref RenderersOpenGL "OpenGL Backend"
 *
 * See @ref Renderers "Interstellar Renderers module".
 * 
 * @subsection input_sec Input
 * Core input module (keyboard, mouse, backends, helpers).
 * - Devices: IKeyboard, IMouse
 * - Frame lifecycle: pump, beginFrame, endFrame
 * - Backends: GLFW (with callback chaining), Null
 *
 * See @ref Input "Interstellar Input module".
 *
 * @subsection io_sec IO
 * IO utilities for engine code.
 * - Error types and expected shim
 * - Filesystem helpers
 * - Containers and file orchestration
 *
 * See @ref IO "Interstellar IO module".
 *
 * @subsection logging_sec Logging
 * Category-based logging facade over spdlog with fmt-safe formatting.
 * - API surface: @ref Interstellar::Logging::Logger and @ref logging_api helpers
 * - Severity: @ref Interstellar::Logging::LogLevel with @ref Interstellar::Logging::ToString
 *   and parsers (@ref Interstellar::Logging::TryParseLogLevel,
 *   @ref Interstellar::Logging::ParseLogLevelOr, @ref Interstellar::Logging::ParseLogLevel)
 * - Category instances: logGeneric, logInit, logConfig, logGraphic
 * - Thread-safety: logging calls are thread-safe
 * - Flushing: errors flush promptly; destructor flushes as safeguard
 * - Files: default `./logs`; override with `INTERSTELLAR_LOG_DIR`
 * - Advanced: access underlying spdlog via `Logger::GetSpdLogger()`
 *
 * See @ref Logging "Interstellar Logging module".
 *
 * @subsection units_sec Units
 * Strongly-typed SI quantities and physical constants (mp-units).
 * - Base constants: gravity, pressure, gas constant, Stefan-Boltzmann
 * - Earth refs: radius, mass, GM
 * - Solar constant
 *
 * See @ref Units "Interstellar Units module".
 *
 * @section examples_sec Examples
 * For quick starts and snippets, visit @ref Examples "Examples".
 */
